  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "note-states",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -330,
      "w": 640,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "ONE SCREEN, FOUR STATES. Most tools show the same dense screen whether you are on fire or not; this one changes shape with the situation.\n\n1 QUIET \u2014 small and boring. Nothing is alerting, here is what changed in the last day, here is the inventory. No charts, because there is nothing to scan for.\n\n2 ONE INCIDENT \u2014 the screen becomes one thing, in the order an operator needs it: what is wrong, what changed, how far it reaches, what proves it, what to do.\n\n3 SEVERAL \u2014 heroes do not stack; that is a dashboard again. The one you should work on is expanded, the rest are a ranked list you can decide from without opening. The ordering rule is printed on the screen, because an operator who cannot see why this is first will not trust that it is: unacknowledged first, then blast radius, then severity, then age. An unacknowledged warning outranks a critical someone is already on.\n\n4 SEVERAL THAT ARE ONE \u2014 the dangerous case."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-grouping",
      "x": 680,
      "y": -330,
      "w": 620,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "GROUPING IS THE RISKIEST THING THIS SCREEN DOES.\n\nThree ways it goes wrong. Genuinely separate incidents shown as one, and you chase a cause that explains half of it. One incident split in two, and you fix a symptom while the cause keeps running. Or forty alerts from a network partition shown as forty incidents.\n\nSo grouping is never silent. Every incident states WHY its alerts were grouped \u2014 'deploy adjacency + 6-minute onset window' \u2014 and carries a one-click 'Not related?' to split it. State 4 is the same idea at its limit: eleven connections went quiet in 90 seconds, and the screen leads with the shape rather than a confident cause. Its evidence is what they share and what they do not, its confidence is Medium not High, and it says plainly that root monitors connections and not the network between them, so it cannot confirm a partition \u2014 only that every collector on one subnet stopped together and the one off it did not.\n\nThe exception carries as much of the argument as the rule: status-page is the only connection not on that subnet and the only one still reporting."
    },
    {
      "id": "note-current",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 540,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "WHAT IS BUILT TODAY \u2014 the screens as implemented and running, for comparison with the proposal on the first page. The Overview here is the one under discussion: it answers 'what' well and 'why' not at all."
    }
  ],
